## A chat is still just text

<At levels="simple">

A model trained on web text, a <GlossaryPopover term="base-model">base model</GlossaryPopover>,
just continues whatever it's given. A chat app writes the whole conversation out as one piece of
text, with special markers for who's speaking, and lets the model continue it. That format is the <GlossaryPopover term="chat-template">chat template</GlossaryPopover>.
Here's the real one the Qwen2.5 chat models use:<Cite id="qwen2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A model trained on web text is a <GlossaryPopover term="base-model">base model</GlossaryPopover>: it continues whatever it's given.
So how does a chat app get answers out of one? It writes the conversation out as a single piece of
text, in a fixed format with special marker tokens, and lets the model continue it. That format is
the <GlossaryPopover term="chat-template">chat template</GlossaryPopover>. Here is the real one used
by the Qwen2.5 chat models:<Cite id="qwen2024" />

</At>

<ChatTemplate />

<At levels="simple">

The text ends by opening the assistant's turn, so the likeliest continuation is an answer. When
the model writes the end marker, the app stops and shows the reply.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The conversation ends with the marker that opens the assistant's turn, so the most likely
continuation is an answer. When the model writes `<|im_end|>`, the chat app stops the loop
from <ChapterRef to="generating-text" /> and shows the reply.

</At>

## Base model or assistant?

Here are the same prompts given to three models: GPT-2; Qwen2.5-0.5B, a small base model from 2024;
and Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct, the same model after tuning it to be an assistant.

<BaseVsInstruct />

<At levels="simple">

Notice that the base models just continue the text: sometimes they answer, and sometimes they
write a quiz or repeat themselves. The tuned model answers and stops, but only when it gets its
chat format.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A few things to notice:

- **Base models continue text.** Asked how to boil an egg, the Qwen base model writes another
  question and a multiple-choice quiz, the way that question might continue on a web page. GPT-2
  repeats itself.
- **Modern base models already know a lot.** The Qwen base model answers the capital question and
  writes a haiku. On the web, a question is often followed by its answer, so an answer is a likely
  continuation.
- **The format matters.** The tuned model, given the bare question without its chat template,
  answers and then keeps going: about checking the answer with Python code, or with a stray line of
  its own template. In its chat format, it answers and stops.

</At>

## How the tuning works

Turning a base model into an assistant usually takes two more rounds of training, after the long
first one. Step through them:

<TrainingStages />

<At levels="simple">

A 2022 paper found that people preferred the answers of a small tuned model over those of a
model more than 100 times bigger that it was built from.<Cite id="ouyang2022" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A 2022 paper on InstructGPT describes this recipe. People preferred the answers of a
1.3-billion-weight tuned model over those of the 175-billion-weight GPT-3 it was built from.<Cite id="ouyang2022" />
Qwen2.5's tuning used over a million examples in the second stage, followed by several stages of
the third.<Cite id="qwen2024" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The three stages, written down

The second stage is the same next-token loss as pretraining, counted only on the example answers.
The third trains a **reward model** $r$ to score an answer $y$ to a prompt $x$, from pairs where
people preferred $y_w$ over $y_l$:

$$
L_{\text{reward}} = -\log \sigma\!\left(r(x, y_w) - r(x, y_l)\right)
$$

where $\sigma(z) = 1 / (1 + e^{-z})$ turns the gap between the two scores into a chance. The loss
is small when the preferred answer scores clearly higher. Then the assistant itself is trained to
earn high scores without drifting far from the tuned model it started as:

$$
\text{maximize} \quad r(x, y) - \beta \, \log \frac{\pi(y \mid x)}{\pi_{\text{SFT}}(y \mid x)}
$$

where $\pi$ is the model being trained, $\pi_{\text{SFT}}$ is the model after the second stage, and
$\beta$ sets how far it may drift. Without that last term, the model learns to please the reward
model in ways people wouldn't.<Cite id="ouyang2022" />

</At>

## Tuning for your own job

<At levels="simple">

Tuning is just more training, and it works for any job: <GlossaryPopover term="fine-tuning">fine-tuning</GlossaryPopover>
a model on your own examples teaches it your task and wording. Two tricks make it cheaper. <GlossaryPopover term="lora">LoRA</GlossaryPopover>
trains a small add-on instead of changing every weight.<Cite id="hu2022" /> <GlossaryPopover term="distillation">Distillation</GlossaryPopover>
trains a small model to copy a big one.<Cite id="hinton2015" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Tuning is just more training, the same measure-and-nudge loop as in <ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />, and it works for
any job, not only chat. <GlossaryPopover term="fine-tuning">Fine-tuning</GlossaryPopover> a model on
your own examples, such as support questions with the replies you'd want, teaches it your task,
format, and wording. Two tricks make it cheaper:

- **Train a small add-on.** Changing every weight of a big model takes memory for all of them and a
  full copy of the model for each job. <GlossaryPopover term="lora">LoRA</GlossaryPopover> leaves
  the weights alone and trains two thin tables next to some of the big tables of weights; their
  product is added to the big table. GPT-2 small turns a token's vector into its query with a
  768 × 768 table: 589,824 weights. Two thin tables, 768 × 8 and 8 × 768, hold 12,288 numbers,
  about 2% as many. On GPT-3, LoRA trained 10,000 times fewer numbers than changing every weight,
  needed a third of the memory, and did about as well.<Cite id="hu2022" />
- **Learn from a bigger model.** <GlossaryPopover term="distillation">Distillation</GlossaryPopover>
  trains a small model, the student, to copy a big one, the teacher. The student can learn from the
  teacher's whole list of probabilities (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />), not just from which token came next,
  so it also learns which other answers were nearly right.<Cite id="hinton2015" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

In symbols, LoRA replaces a frozen table $W$ (here $d \times d$) with

$$
W' = W + \frac{\alpha}{r} B A
$$

where $B$ is $d \times r$, $A$ is $r \times d$, $r$ is the small rank (8 above), and $\alpha$ is a
fixed scale. Only $A$ and $B$ are trained. $B$ starts at all zeros, so training starts from exactly
the original model.<Cite id="hu2022" />

Distillation softens both models' probabilities with a temperature $T$ above 1, so the near misses
show, and trains the student's $q^{(T)}$ to match the teacher's $p^{(T)}$:

$$
L = -T^2 \sum_{i} p_i^{(T)} \log q_i^{(T)}
$$

where the $T^2$ keeps the slopes at the same size whatever $T$ is.<Cite id="hinton2015" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A chat is written out as one text in a chat template, and the model continues it.
- A base model just continues text; tuning teaches it to answer in the chat format and stop.
- Tuning has two usual stages after pretraining: learning from example answers, then from people's
  preferences between answers.
- Fine-tuning trains a model further for your own task. LoRA trains a small add-on instead of every
  weight; distillation trains a small model to copy a big one's probabilities.

<At levels="deep">

- Preference tuning trains a reward model with $-\log \sigma(r_w - r_l)$; LoRA adds a trained
  $BA$ to a frozen $W$.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** even tuned assistants sometimes make things up or trip over simple questions. Why?
